2147483648`, `-2147483649`, `12345678901234567890`, randomized decimal renderings | [ ] |\n| C40 | `cJSON_Parse` | numbers with junk-but-accepted `strtod` prefixes: `0x10`, `01`, `1.2.3`, `1e`, `1e+`, `--1`, `-+1`, `1-2` (each consumed partially — top-level parse then succeeds or fails exactly like C) | [ ] |\n| C41 | `cJSON_Parse` | strings: empty, all 7 escapes, `\\/`, `A`, `é`, `€`, `😀` (surrogate pair), `\u0000`, raw UTF-8 bytes, 8-bit bytes | [ ] |\n| C42 | `cJSON_Parse` | arrays: `[]`, `[1]`, `[1,2,3]`, whitespace variants `[ 1 , 2 ]`, nested `[[[]]]`, mixed types, randomized | [ ] |\n| C43 | `cJSON_Parse` | objects: `{}`, one member, many members, duplicate keys, whitespace variants, nested, keys with escapes, randomized | [ ] |\n| C44 | `cJSON_Parse` | leading/trailing whitespace, `\\t\\r\\n`, all bytes ≤ 32 as whitespace | [ ] |\n| C45 | `cJSON_Parse` | UTF-8 BOM prefix (`EF BB BF`) + value; BOM alone; BOM with < 4 accessible bytes (`skip_utf8_bom` needs `can_access_at_index(buffer,4)`) | [ ] |\n| C46 | `cJSON_Parse` | trailing garbage after a complete value (`\"1 garbage\"`, `\"{} x\"`) — accepted, `cJSON_Parse` does not require NUL termination | [ ] |\n| C47 | `cJSON_ParseWithLength` | `buffer_length` = `strlen+1` (canonical) | [ ] |\n| C48 | `cJSON_ParseWithLength` | `buffer_length` = `strlen` (no visible NUL) — changes `can_read`/`can_access_at_index` decisions for `null`/`true`/`false`/numbers at the very end of the buffer | [ ] |\n| C49 | `cJSON_ParseWithLength` | `buffer_length` shorter than the value (truncation at every offset of a fixed document) | [ ] |\n| C50 | `cJSON_ParseWithLength` | `buffer_length` longer than the string (reads past the NUL) | [ ] |\n| C51 | `cJSON_ParseWithOpts` | `require_null_terminated` = 0, `return_parse_end` = NULL | [ ] |\n| C52 | `cJSON_ParseWithOpts` | `require_null_terminated` = 0, `return_parse_end` != NULL — compare the returned **offset** for success and failure inputs | [ ] |\n| C53 | `cJSON_ParseWithOpts` | `require_null_terminated` = 1 (and 2/-1 truthy) on clean and on trailing-garbage input | [ ] |\n| C54 | `cJSON_ParseWithLengthOpts` | full cross product: length ∈ {exact, exact+1, short} × `require_null_terminated` ∈ {0,1} × `return_parse_end` ∈ {NULL, ptr} | [ ] |\n| C55 | `cJSON_Parse` | nesting depth 1, 2, 998, 999 (accepted) for both `[` and `{` | [ ] |\n| C56 | `cJSON_Parse` | nesting depth 1000, 1001, 2000 (rejected at `CJSON_NESTING_LIMIT`) | [ ] |\n| C57 | `cJSON_GetErrorPtr` | after a successful parse (reset) and after each failing parse (offset check) | [ ] |\n| C58 | `cJSON_GetArraySize` | array with 0 / 1 / many elements; object; non-container item; `NULL` | [ ] |\n| C59 | `cJSON_GetArrayItem` | index 0, middle, last, last+1, large, on arrays of size 0/1/many; also on objects | [ ] |\n| C60 | `cJSON_GetObjectItem` | exact key, differently-cased key, absent key, empty-string key, key on an array (children without `string`) | [ ] |\n| C61 | `cJSON_GetObjectItemCaseSensitive` | same inputs as C60 | [ ] |\n| C62 | `cJSON_HasObjectItem` | present / absent / differently-cased | [ ] |\n| C63 | `cJSON_GetStringValue` | string item, string-reference item, non-string items | [ ] |\n| C64 | `cJSON_GetNumberValue` | number item (incl. NaN/inf payloads — compared as bit patterns), non-number items | [ ] |\n| C65 | `cJSON_Is*` (10 fns) | every function × every one of the 8 types × reference/const-key variants × invalid types (`0`, `3`, `0x0F`, `0xFF`, `0x1FF`, `INT_MIN`) | [ ] |\n| C66 | `cJSON_SetNumberHelper` | number classes of C9 on a number item; check `valueint`/`valuedouble` and the return value | [ ] |\n| C67 | `cJSON_SetValuestring` | new string shorter than / equal to / longer than the old one, on a heap string item; then print | [ ] |\n| C68 | `cJSON_DetachItemViaPointer` | detach first / middle / last / only element of an array and of an object; then print both the parent and the detached item | [ ] |\n| C69 | `cJSON_DetachItemFromArray` | `which` = 0, middle, last, last+1 on sizes 1/2/5 | [ ] |\n| C70 | `cJSON_DetachItemFromObject` / `…CaseSensitive` | exact and differently-cased key, first/middle/last member | [ ] |\n| C71 | `cJSON_DeleteItemFromArray` | index 0, middle, last, out of range | [ ] |\n| C72 | `cJSON_DeleteItemFromObject` / `…CaseSensitive` | present / absent / differently-cased key | [ ] |\n| C73 | `cJSON_InsertItemInArray` | `which` = 0 (front), middle, last, `size` (⇒ append), `size+3` on sizes 0/1/2/5 | [ ] |\n| C74 | `cJSON_ReplaceItemViaPointer` | replace first / middle / last / only element of an array and of an object | [ ] |\n| C75 | `cJSON_ReplaceItemInArray` | `which` = 0, middle, last, out of range | [ ] |\n| C76 | `cJSON_ReplaceItemInObject` / `…CaseSensitive` | exact / differently-cased / absent key; replacement with and without an existing (const or heap) key | [ ] |\n| C77 | `cJSON_Duplicate` | `recurse` = 0 on every type (children must **not** be copied) | [ ] |\n| C78 | `cJSON_Duplicate` | `recurse` = 1 (and 2/-1) on nested arrays/objects, incl. `StringIsConst` keys and `cJSON_IsReference` items (flag is cleared) | [ ] |\n| C79 | `cJSON_Duplicate_rec` | called directly with `depth` = 0, 1, 9998, 9999, 10000 × `recurse` 0/1 | [ ] |\n| C80 | `cJSON_Compare` | `case_sensitive` = 0 and 1 × equal / unequal pairs of every type | [ ] |\n| C81 | `cJSON_Compare` | numbers within/outside the `DBL_EPSILON` relative tolerance (incl. 0 vs 0, -0.0 vs 0.0, NaN vs NaN, inf vs inf) | [ ] |\n| C82 | `cJSON_Compare` | nested arrays (same/different length, same/different order) | [ ] |\n| C83 | `cJSON_Compare` | objects: same members different order; differently-cased keys with `case_sensitive` 0 vs 1; `a` ⊂ `b`; `b` ⊂ `a` | [ ] |\n| C84 | `cJSON_Compare` | identical pointer (`a == b`), and self-comparison of an invalid-typed item | [ ] |\n| C85 | `cJSON_Minify` | plain whitespace; `//` comment (with and without terminating newline); `/* */` comment (terminated and unterminated); lone `/`; strings containi