{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"ad29a01141d96e031","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_015QLaMewRYGcNk9oQBxZgNE","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-DhAaWy/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-DhAaWy-translated-rust/99d8c152-90eb-46f7-8482-a09fedc8e20a/tasks/ad29a01141d96e031.output","summary":"**NO DIVERGENCES FOUND** in lines 1–1330 of `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-DhAaWy/translated_rust/c_src/cJSON.c` vs the corresponding functions in `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-DhAaWy/translated_rust/src/lib.rs`.\n\n## What I checked (statement-by-statement, all 37 in-scope items)\n\n`global_error`, `cJSON_GetErrorPtr`, `cJSON_GetStringValue`, `cJSON_GetNumberValue`, `cJSON_Version`, `case_insensitive_strcmp`, `internal_hooks`/`global_hooks`, `cJSON_strdup`, `cJSON_InitHooks`, `cJSON_New_Item`, `cJSON_Delete`, `get_decimal_point`, `parse_buffer` + `can_read`/`can_access_at_index`/`cannot_access_at_index`/`buffer_at_offset`, `parse_number`, `cJSON_SetNumberHelper`, `cJSON_SetValuestring`, `printbuffer`, `ensure`, `update_offset`, `compare_double`, `print_number`, `parse_hex4`, `utf16_literal_to_utf8`, `parse_string`, `print_string_ptr`, `print_string`, `buffer_skip_whitespace`, `skip_utf8_bom`, `cJSON_ParseWithOpts`, `cJSON_ParseWithLengthOpts`, `cJSON_Parse`, `cJSON_ParseWithLength`, `print`, `cJSON_Print`, `cJSON_PrintUnformatted`, `cJSON_PrintBuffered`, `cJSON_PrintPreallocated`. Also verified `cJSON` / `cJSON_Hooks` layout against `c_src/cJSON.h` (56-byte struct, same field order) and the extern decls in `src/ffi.rs`.\n\nI also mechanically compared the multiset of integer literals in C lines 77–1330 against Rust lines 174–1410: every constant matches, including all `sizeof(\"\")`→`1` and `sizeof(\"\\\"\\\"\")`→`3` expansions, `INT_MAX`/`INT_MAX/2`, `26`, `15`, `256`, the UTF-16 constants (`0xD800/0xDBFF/0xDC00/0xDFFF/0x3FF/0x10000/0x10FFFF`), the UTF-8 masks (`0x80/0x800/0xC0/0xE0/0xF0/0xBF/0x7F/0xFF`), and `can_access_at_index(buffer, 4)` in `skip_utf8_bom`.\n\n## Empirical confirmation\n\nThe prebuilt `c_src/build/libcjson.so.1.7.19` (built with `-std=c89 -pedantic -DENABLE_LOCALES`) and `target/release/libcJSON_test.so` are both current. I `dlopen`ed both side-by-side (RTLD_LOCAL) in throwaway harnesses under `$TMPDIR` (no repo files touched) and got zero mismatches on:\n\n1. ~110 hand-picked parse cases × {`ParseWithOpts`, `ParseWithLengthOpts` at len-1/len/len+1/0} × `require_null_terminated` ∈ {0,1}, comparing return-nullness, `*return_parse_end` offset, `cJSON_GetErrorPtr()` offset, `Print`, `PrintUnformatted`, `PrintBuffered(0..8)`, and `PrintPreallocated(0..40)` byte-for-byte including untouched buffer tail.\n2. ~240k randomized inputs over a JSON-hostile alphabet (BOM bytes, `\\`, `u`, hex, control chars), same comparisons; repeated under `C`, `de_DE.utf8` and `ru_RU.utf8` (comma decimal point) to exercise `get_decimal_point` / `parse_number`'s `.`→locale substitution / `print_number`'s reverse substitution.\n3. 20k random 64-bit bit patterns reinterpreted as doubles through `CreateNumber`+`PrintUnformatted` and `valueint`, plus explicit `cJSON_SetNumberHelper` saturation boundaries (`±2147483647/8/9`, `±1e300`, `±inf`, `0.0/0.0`) — the `double_to_int` NaN→`INT_MIN` shim reproduces `cvttsd2si` exactly.\n4. All 65536 `\"\\uXXXX\"` escapes, a sweep of surrogate pairs (`0xD800..0xDBFF` × `0xDBF0..0xE010`), all 255 byte values inside a string for `print_string_ptr`.\n5. `ensure()` growth: arrays/strings of 1…4095 elements forcing repeated reallocation, plus `PrintBuffered` prebuffer sweeps.\n6. `cJSON_InitHooks` reallocate-selection, verified by interposing counted `malloc`/`free`/`realloc` in the executable: all 8 hook combinations produce identical (malloc, free, realloc) call counts, so the Rust `fn_addr_alloc(...) == malloc as usize` comparison behaves exactly like C's `global_hooks.allocate == malloc` across the DSO boundary.\n7. Allocation-failure injection (fail on the k-th allocation, k = 0…59) for 11 inputs, comparing parse result, error position and printed output — i.e. all `goto fail` cleanup paths in `parse_number`, `parse_string`, `ensure`, `print` and `cJSON_ParseWithLengthOpts` are equivalent.\n\n## Notable non-obvious details the translation gets right\n\n- `src/lib.rs:56` `NAN_0_DIV_0 = 0xFFF8_0000_0000_0000`. Because `-ftrapping-math` is on by default, GCC does **not** constant-fold cJSON's `#define NAN 0.0/0.0`, so `cJSON_GetNumberValue` on a non-number executes a runtime `divsd` yielding the x86 QNaN indefinite with the **sign bit set**. Measured: C returns `fff8000000000000`, Rust returns `fff8000000000000` (Rust's own `f64::NAN` would have been `7ff8...` — a real divergence, correctly avoided).\n- `sprintf` → `snprintf` substitutions (`cJSON_Version` size 15, `print_number` size 26, `print_string_ptr` `\"u%04x\"` size 6) are all sized so truncation is impossible, and `snprintf`'s return value equals `sprintf`'s would-be length, so `print_number`'s `length > (int)(sizeof(number_buffer)-1)` check keeps identical control flow.\n- `double_to_int` (`src/lib.rs:153`) exists precisely because NaN falls through both saturation guards in `parse_number`/`cJSON_SetNumberHelper` (all NaN comparisons are false) and C's `(int)NaN` is `INT_MIN`, not Rust's saturating `0`.\n- `parse_hex4` reproduces C's unsigned promotion order exactly: `(c as u32).wrapping_sub(b'0' as u32)` and `10u32.wrapping_add(c as u32).wrapping_sub(b'A' as u32)` mirror `(unsigned int)input[i] - '0'` and `(unsigned int)10 + input[i] - 'A'`.\n- `c\"\\xEF\\xBB\\xBF\"` in `skip_utf8_bom` is a byte-exact 3-byte BOM (Rust C-string literals permit `\\x80`–`\\xFF`, unlike normal `&str` literals).\n\nThe only behavioural differences I could construct at all are unreachable through any public API: `hooks_allocate`/`hooks_deallocate` return `null_mut()`/no-op when a hook is `None`, whereas C would call through a NULL function pointer and crash. `cJSON_InitHooks` always installs non-NULL `allocate`/`deallocate`, so no caller can reach that state.","usage":{"total_tokens":120330,"tool_uses":20,"duration_ms":665935},"uuid":"e1ea1ee4-23e3-4ff6-96d5-4fbf5eafbece","session_id":"99d8c152-90eb-46f7-8482-a09fedc8e20a"}
