istic differences.","agentId":"abdf14a8ce1fb21f0","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I audited the assigned range statement-by-statement and then validated the reading with a large differential test campaign against the C reference `.so`.\n\n## NO DIVERGENCES FOUND\n\n### Manual comparison (all in-scope functions mapped and checked)\n\n| C (cJSON.c) | Rust (src/lib.rs) | verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| `parse_value` 1330-1382 | 1412-1477 | identical, including the `can_read(4/5/4)` order and `strncmp` widths |\n| `print_value` 1385-1456 | 1480-1544 | `switch (type & 0xFF)` → Rust const-pattern `match` with `_ => FALSE` default; `raw_length = strlen+sizeof(\"\")` → `strlen+1` |\n| `parse_array` 1459-1554 / `parse_object` 1619-1734 | 1547-1639 / 1698-1813 | `goto success`/`goto fail` correctly modelled as nested `'fail: { 'success: { … } /*success*/ } /*fail*/`; `do{}while` → `loop { … if !cond break }` is equivalent because the C loop body always executes once; `head->prev = current_item` bookkeeping, the `offset--` steps, and the `cannot_access_at_index(input_buffer, 1)` check in `parse_object` all match; `cJSON_Delete(head)` only on the fail path in both |\n| `print_array` 1557-1616 / `print_object` 1737-1848 | 1642-1695 / 1816-1929 | identical, including the quirky `ensure(output_buffer, length)` (no `+1`) for the `:` separator, `ensure(depth)` for tabs, and `depth-1` closing tabs |\n| `cJSON_GetArraySize`, `get_array_item`, `cJSON_GetArrayItem`, `get_object_item`, `cJSON_GetObjectItem(CaseSensitive)`, `cJSON_HasObjectItem` | 1933-2041 | identical, incl. the `string != NULL` guard inside the case-sensitive loop only, and the trailing `string == NULL → NULL` |\n| `suffix_object`, `create_reference`, `add_item_to_array`, `cast_away_const`, `add_item_to_object`, `cJSON_AddItemToObject(CS)`, `cJSON_AddItemReferenceTo*`, all nine `cJSON_Add*ToObject` | 2044-2325 | identical pointer-update order; the `if (child->prev)` \"silently drop & leak\" quirk is reproduced |\n| `cJSON_DetachItemViaPointer` / `FromArray` / `FromObject(CaseSensitive)`, `cJSON_DeleteItemFrom*` | 2328-2411 | identical |\n| `cJSON_InsertItemInArray`, `cJSON_ReplaceItemViaPointer`, `cJSON_ReplaceItemInArray`, `replace_item_in_object`, `cJSON_ReplaceItemInObject(CaseSensitive)` | 2415-2546 | identical, incl. corrupted-item early returns and the `parent->child->prev == parent->child` self-loop case |\n| every `cJSON_Create*`, `cJSON_Create{Int,Float,Double,String}Array` | 2550-2839 | identical; `for(i=0; a && i<(size_t)count; i++)` → `while !a.is_null() && i < count as usize`; `a->child->prev = n` after the loop; INT saturation matches (`double_to_int` reproduces x86-64 `cvttsd2si` = `INT_MIN` for NaN) |\n| `cJSON_Duplicate`, `cJSON_Duplicate_rec` | 2843-2929 | identical, incl. `CJSON_CIRCULAR_LIMIT` check placement and the `StringIsConst` key aliasing |\n| `skip_oneline_comment`, `skip_multiline_comment`, `minify_string`, `cJSON_Minify` | 2931-3018 | the C `for(; c; ++(*input))` increments are correctly re-placed at the end of the Rust loop bodies, after the early `return`s; `minify_string`'s escaped-quote branch does its own `+1` and then falls into the loop increment (net `+2`) in both |\n| all ten `cJSON_Is*`, `cJSON_Compare`, `cJSON_malloc`, `cJSON_free` | 3021-3269 | identical, incl. `0xff` (lowercase) in `IsTrue`, the two-pass `cJSON_ArrayForEach` expansion `a_element = if !a.is_null() { (*a).child } else { null_mut() }`, and both `default: return false` arms |\n| `test.c` `print_preallocated`, `create_objects`, `driver` | src/test_driver.rs | identical; `volatile double zero` → `core::ptr::read_volatile`, so `1.0/zero` is computed at runtime as `+inf`; `EXIT_FAILURE` → `exit(1)`; `int numbers[3][3]` → `*const [c_int; 3]` |\n\nOverflow behaviour is safe: `[profile.release]` has no `overflow-checks`, so Rust's `-= 1` wraps like C's `size_t--`, and I verified no in-scope subtraction can underflow anyway (`parse_array`/`parse_object` always have `offset >= 1` at the `offset -= 1` sites; `print_object` always has `depth >= 1`).\n\n### Differential validation (all byte-identical)\n- 40 000 random-garbage parses (`ParseWithOpts` + `GetErrorPtr` offset + `Print`/`PrintUnformatted`/`PrintBuffered`/`Duplicate`/`Compare`/`PrintPreallocated` at ±1 of the exact size).\n- 30 000 generated *valid* JSON documents plus a random truncation of each; round-tripped through print → parse → print and through `Minify` → parse.\n- 40 000 `cJSON_Minify` fuzz cases + every leading byte 0x01-0xFF + hand-picked pathological comment/escape cases, comparing the **whole 600-byte buffer** (bytes past the NUL included).\n- 3 000 × 120-step structural fuzz over 26 mutation ops, dumping `type/string/valuestring/valueint/valuedouble`, every `prev`/`next` null-ness and the `head->prev == tail` invariant after each step.\n- `cJSON_PrintPreallocated` for 12 documents × formatted/unformatted × every buffer length 0..220, comparing 300 raw bytes each (3 MB of output, identical).\n- Allocation-failure injection via `cJSON_InitHooks` for 5 scenarios × 82 different \"fail on the Nth malloc\" points, comparing return values **and the malloc/free counters** — leak/free behaviour matches exactly on every failure path.\n- Nesting limit boundaries (arrays and objects at depth 998..1002), `CJSON_CIRCULAR_LIMIT` boundary (`Duplicate` at depth 9998..10002), 999-deep formatted print (1 006 996 bytes, identical hash), 5 000-sibling lists, corrupted-list inputs (`child->prev == NULL`), NULL-key object members, all reference types, key re-assignment across objects, and 35 bogus `type` values × 35 `cJSON_Compare` pairs.\n- `driver()` stdout compared against `c_src/build/libcJSON_test.so` — identical, including the `\"number\": null` line produced by `1.0/zero`.\n\n### One non-behavioural note (not a semantic divergence)\n`cJSON_Duplicate_rec` (C line 2734, Rust `src/lib.rs:2847-2848`) has external linkage in C but is not `CJSON_PUBLIC`, so the reference build (`-fvisibility=hidden`, CMakeLists.txt:89) does **not** export it; the Rust translation marks it `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub`, so it *is* exported. `dlsym(h, \"cJSON_Duplicate_rec\")` returns NULL for the C `.so` and a valid pointer for the Rust `.so`. This is already documented in `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-DhAaWy/translated_rust/SYMBOLS.md:30-36`; the function's behaviour itself is identical (verified at depths 9998-10001). Apart from `driver` (which the C build ships in a separate `.so`), the exported symbol sets are otherwise identical."}],"resolvedModel":"global.anthropic.claude-opus-5[1m]","totalDurationMs":1597498,"totalTokens":185286,"totalToolUseCount":37,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3515,"cache_read_input_tokens":177672,"output_tokens":4097,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1184},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3515},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":5,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":32,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
